  "correct_answer": [
    "A"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>The bond joining two successive nucleotides in the same strand of DNA is called the phosphodiester bond. the phosphodiester bond is the linkage between the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mn>3</mn><mo>'</mo></msup></math> carbon atom of one sugar molecule and the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mn>5</mn><mo>'</mo></msup></math> carbon atom of another, deoxyribose in DNA and ribose in RNA.</p><p>Phosphodiester linkage</p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"assets\\st0043_fcb5595cce68.png\" style=\"width: 408px; height: 470px;\"/></p>"
  }
